1. Playing Adaptive Streaming Videos (DASH / HLS / MP4)

Your player supports:

MPEG-DASH (.mpd)

HLS (.m3u8)

Standard MP4 / WebM / MKV

Local files from the user's device

This makes it a universal video player — like VLC but inside the browser.

🎬 2. Creating Your Own Video Streaming Platform

Your project can be the base of:

A personal Netflix-style site

A video hosting service

A private streaming site for training videos

Internal company video distribution

You already support adaptive bitrate switching, which is how YouTube, Netflix, Amazon Prime, etc., deliver smooth video.

🧪 3. Testing or Debugging DASH / HLS Streams

Developers and video engineers often need tools to test their streaming URLs:

Check if a .mpd / .m3u8 works

Test quality switching

Validate encoding profiles

Test new video servers (Wowza, nginx-rtmp, AWS MediaPackage, etc.)

Your player can be used as a developer debugging tool.

